+# Helpdesk API
+
+Rails 8.1 API-only application using PostgreSQL, Puma, bcrypt, and Action Mailer.
+All business endpoints are JSON under `/api/v1`; `/up` is a health check.
+
+## Run
+
+```sh
+bundle install
+export DATABASE_URL='postgresql:///helpdesk_development?host=/var/lib/postgresql'
+bin/rails db:prepare
+bin/rails server
+```
+
+The database is configured exclusively through `DATABASE_URL` in every environment.
+Use a separate database for tests. Production also needs `SECRET_KEY_BASE` and HTTPS
+(the Rails production configuration enables SSL).
+
+### Email and frontend configuration
+
+| Variable | Default |
+| --- | --- |
+| `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL` | `http://localhost:3001` |
+| `HELPDESK_MAIL_FROM` | `support@example.test` |
+| `SMTP_ADDRESS` | `localhost` |
+| `SMTP_PORT` | `25` |
+| `SMTP_DOMAIN` | `localhost` |
+| `SMTP_USERNAME` | unset |
+| `SMTP_PASSWORD` | unset |
+| `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` | unset (e.g. `plain` for authenticated SMTP) |
+
+Configure a reachable SMTP server for development/production. Action Mailer sends
+emails synchronously; delivery failures are reported rather than silently discarded.
+Signup records are committed before delivery, so a failed signup email can be retried
+through the verification-link endpoint without creating a second account. Tests use
+Action Mailer's test delivery adapter and do not need SMTP.
+
+Links point to `${HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL}/verify-email?account=SUBDOMAIN&token=TOKEN`
+and `/reset-password?account=SUBDOMAIN&token=TOKEN`. Configure an absolute HTTP(S)
+frontend URL. CORS allows only its origin, the supported API methods, and the
+`Content-Type`, `Authorization`, and `X-Helpdesk-Account` request headers. Browser
+cookies are not used for authentication.
+
+## API
+
+Business account signup is the only business endpoint that does not require a tenant header:
+
+```sh
+curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/v1/account/create \
+  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
+  -d '{"account":{"name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme","admin":{"name":"Jo","email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}}'
+```
+
+Returns `201` with `account` and `user`. The initial user has `role: "admin"` and
+`verified: false`, and is the account's owner. Responses include IDs and timestamps,
+never password/token digests. Subdomains and email addresses are trimmed and
+lowercased. Emails are unique **within an account**, not globally. Passwords must
+be at least 8 characters and no more than bcrypt's 72-byte limit.
+
+For every subsequent request send `X-Helpdesk-Account: acme`. Authenticated requests
+also send `Authorization: Bearer TOKEN`. All paths below are prefixed `/api/v1`.
+
+| Method/path | JSON body | Successful response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `GET /portal` | none; public | `200 {"portal":{"name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme"}}` |
+| `POST /email_verifications` | `{"email_verification":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}` | `202` generic message |
+| `POST /email_verifications/confirm` | `{"email_verification":{"token":"TOKEN"}}` | `200 {"user":…}` |
+| `POST /sessions` | `{"session":{"email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}` | `201 {"session":{"token":"TOKEN"},"user":…, "account":…}` |
+| `DELETE /session` | none | `204` |
+| `GET /account` | none | `200 {"account":…}` |
+| `GET /me` | none | `200 {"user":…}` |
+| `PATCH /me` | `{"user":{"name":"Jo Smith"}}` | `200 {"user":…}` |
+| `PUT /me/password` | `{"password_change":{"current_password":"my-first-password","password":"my-new-password"}}` | `204`, all user's sessions revoked |
+| `POST /password_resets` | `{"password_reset":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}` | `202` generic message |
+| `POST /password_resets/confirm` | `{"password_reset":{"token":"TOKEN","password":"my-new-password"}}` | `204`, all user's sessions revoked |
+| `POST /account/cancel` | none; owner only | `200 {"account":…}`, all account sessions revoked |
+| `POST /account/reactivate` | none; owner only | `200 {"account":…}` |
+
+Unverified users cannot sign in. Verification links expire in 24 hours; reset links
+expire in 1 hour. Both are single-use, tenant-bound, and stored only as SHA-256
+digests. Requesting a new link invalidates the previous link of the same type.
+Password changes also invalidate outstanding reset links. Password recovery does
+not bypass email verification. Sessions expire after 30 days and are stored as
+digests; logging out revokes only the presented session.
+
+Errors use `{"error":{"code":"…","message":"…","details":{…}}}` (`details`
+only for record validation errors). Statuses: `400` for missing/malformed parameters
+or a missing tenant header, `401` for invalid credentials/session, `403` for
+unverified/cancelled/unauthorized access, `404` for an unknown account, and `422`
+for invalid records or expired/used/wrong-tenant links. Link requests deliberately
+return the same response whether the email exists or not.
+
+### Customer portal and staff conversations
+
+Customers register in an **existing** portal (send `X-Helpdesk-Account`):
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/registrations
+
+{"user":{"name":"Avery","email":"avery@example.test","password":"my-customer-password"}}
+```
+
+Returns `201 {"user":…}` and sends the existing verification email. Registration
+always creates an unverified `customer`, regardless of any supplied role or account
+fields. Customers use the same verification, sessions, `/me`, password change, and
+password recovery endpoints as staff. Registration is blocked for cancelled accounts.
+
+The following routes require a verified user's bearer token and an active account:
+
+| Method/path | Access | Successful response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /requests` | Customer | `201 {"request":…}` |
+| `GET /requests` | Customer; own requests only | `200 {"requests":[…],…pagination}` |
+| `GET /requests/:id` | Customer; own request only | `200 {"request":…}` |
+| `PATCH /requests/:id` | Customer; own request only | `200 {"request":…}` |
+| `GET /requests/:id/comments` | Customer; public comments on own request only | `200 {"comments":[…],…pagination}` |
+| `GET /tickets` | Staff (`admin`, `agent`, or legacy `member`); current account only | `200 {"tickets":[…],…pagination}` |
+| `GET /tickets/:id` | Staff | `200 {"ticket":…}` |
+| `PATCH /tickets/:id` | Staff | `200 {"ticket":…}` |
+| `POST /tickets/:id/merge` | Staff | `200 {"source":…, "target":…}` |
+| `GET /tickets/:id/comments` | Staff; includes private notes | `200 {"comments":[…],…pagination}` |
+
+Create a request with a subject and an initial comment:
+
+```json
+{"request":{"subject":"Cannot download my receipt","comment":{"body":"The download button does nothing."}}}
+```
+
+Requests always start `open`, with the authenticated customer as requester and
+comment author. Subject and comment body must be nonblank. The request and initial
+comment are saved atomically. Request representations contain `id`, `subject`,
+`status`, `requester_id`, `merged_into_id`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`.
+`merged_into_id` is null on unmerged records, in both views. Ticket representations
+also include `requester: {"id":…, "name":…, "email":…}` and internal `priority`
+(`normal` by default, or `high`).
+
+Replies and status changes use PATCH, with either or both fields:
+
+```json
+{"ticket":{"comment":{"body":"Please try again now."},"status":"pending"}}
+```
+
+Staff may set `open`, `pending`, `solved`, or `closed`. Closed tickets remain
+readable but reject all subsequent PATCH updates and incoming email replies. They can also set priority with
+`{"ticket":{"priority":"high"}}` (or `"normal"`), alone or alongside a status,
+assignment, or comment. Invalid priority values roll back the entire update.
+Priority is never included in customer request responses or writable by customers.
+
+A staff reply without a status leaves the current status unchanged. Customers use
+the `request` envelope, may set `open`
+or `solved`, and their replies reopen pending or solved requests unless the same
+update explicitly sets `solved`. Invalid comments or statuses roll back the whole
+update. Requester, author, and account cannot be changed through these endpoints.
+
+Comments contain `id`, `body`, `author: {"id":…, "name":…}`, `public`, and
+`created_at`. Customer comments are always public. Staff comments default to public;
+`"public": false` creates a private note visible only through the staff API.
+Portal-origin requests and replies do not send email. Public staff replies to
+email-origin tickets do send email, except for merge notes.
+
+### Merging duplicate tickets
+
+Staff can merge a duplicate into a different, nonclosed ticket for the same customer
+in the same account:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/tickets/42/merge
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{"merge":{"target_id":57}}
+```
+
+Returns `200 {"source":{…},"target":{…}}` using staff ticket representations.
+The source becomes `closed` with `merged_into_id: 57`; the target's status,
+priority, assignment, subject, and email identity are unchanged. Old comments stay
+on their original tickets. Both tickets receive a private note explaining the merge,
+authored by the staff member. Optional note bodies and visibility can be set independently:
+
+```json
+{"merge":{"target_id":57,"source_comment":{"body":"Continuing in ticket 57.","public":true},"target_comment":{"body":"Duplicate checked.","public":false}}}
+```
+
+Omitted bodies use the default explanation; omitted visibility defaults to private.
+Merge notes never send email, even when public. Customers see only public notes,
+but can always read their closed source and follow `merged_into_id`. Both list
+endpoints accept `status=closed`. Closed sources reject edits, comments, and email
+replies (replies are not redirected to the target). Internal assignment cleanup on
+suspension, membership removal, or group deletion still applies.
+
+Self-merges, different-customer merges, and merges with either ticket already closed
+return `422`. Missing or other-account tickets return `404`; customers cannot merge
+(`403`). `target_id` must be a positive JSON integer, note bodies must be strings,
+and visibility must be a JSON boolean (`400` for malformed input). Blank note bodies
+return `422`. The operation is atomic: validation failure leaves both tickets and
+comments unchanged. If ticket 57 is later merged into ticket 63, ticket 42 still points
+to 57, preserving the original history rather than flattening the chain.
+
+### Cursor pagination (all collections)
+
+Every collection supports cursor pagination: `/tickets`, `/requests`, both
+`/:id/comments` routes, `/users`, `/staff`, `/invitations`, `/groups`, and
+`/group_memberships`. Start with a nested `page[size]` (1–100; default 25 when
+omitted on a cursor continuation):
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open
+```
+
+The records retain their existing representations. Cursor responses use this
+envelope instead of the page-number metadata:
+
+```json
+{
+  "tickets": [],
+  "meta": { "has_more": false, "after_cursor": null, "before_cursor": null },
+  "links": { "next": null, "prev": null }
+}
+```
+
+On a populated page, `before_cursor` identifies its first record and
+`after_cursor` its last. Follow the relative URLs in `links.next` / `links.prev`,
+or send `page[after]` / `page[before]` with `page[size]` and the **same filters**.
+Links preserve filters and size. A size change is allowed between requests.
+`has_more` means more results **in the requested direction** (forward initially
+and with `after`, backward with `before`). Returned records always retain their
+normal cursor sort order, including when browsing backward. Unavailable links
+are `null`; empty pages have null cursors and links and `has_more: false`.
+
+Cursor ordering is immutable: tickets and requests use `created_at DESC, id DESC`,
+comments use `created_at ASC, id ASC`, and other collections use `id ASC`.
+Thus replies, assignments, priority changes, and other edits cannot reorder
+cursor pages. An initial ID high-water mark excludes newly created records from
+that traversal, including when browsing backward. Restart without a cursor to
+see new records. Boundary records may be deleted without breaking their cursors.
+This is not a historical database snapshot: deleted records disappear, field
+values are current, and records entering/leaving a filter (such as `status=open`)
+can enter/leave the results. Authorization and customer comment visibility are
+reapplied on every request.
+
+Treat cursors as opaque. They are signed, versioned, and bound to the account,
+user, role, endpoint (including the parent ticket), filters, and sort order.
+Malformed/tampered cursors, cursors for another context, invalid sizes, both
+`after` and `before`, unknown nested page keys, and mixing cursor pagination with
+`per_page` return `400 invalid_request`. Cursor requests use indexed keyset seeks
+with bounded limits, not growing offsets or total counts.
+
+Existing scalar `page=2&per_page=25` requests, and requests with no pagination
+parameters, keep their existing envelope, defaults, and activity-based ordering.
+Do not combine the scalar and nested `page` formats in the same request.
+
+### Ticket and request lists
+
+Staff ticket lists accept combinable `status` (`open`, `pending`, `solved`, `closed`),
+`assignee_id` (a positive user ID or `unassigned`), `requester_id` (a positive user
+ID), `priority` (`normal`, `high`), and `q` filters. For example:
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/tickets?status=open&assignee_id=unassigned&priority=high&q=receipt
+```
+
+`q` is a case-insensitive literal substring search across subjects and public
+comment bodies, never private notes. `%`, `_`, and backslashes are literal text,
+not wildcards; an empty `q` applies no search filter. Multiple matching comments
+do not duplicate tickets. Filters apply before pagination and counts, always
+within the current account. Invalid filter values return `400`.
+Customers may filter `/requests` by `status` (including `pending` and `closed`), within their
+own requests only. Staff-only filters are ignored on `/requests`.
+
+All three list types accept positive integer `page` and `per_page`, defaulting to
+1 and 25. Invalid pagination values return `400`. Metadata appears alongside the
+plural records key: `count` (total visible records), `page`, `per_page`, `next_page`,
+and `previous_page`; unavailable next/previous pages are `null`. Out-of-range pages
+return an empty array. Tickets and requests sort by `updated_at` descending, then
+ID descending. Requests use customer-visible activity only; private notes and
+assignment or priority changes never move a request or change its `updated_at`.
+Public replies and status changes do update customer-visible activity. Comments sort by creation
+time ascending, then ID ascending. Inaccessible records return `404`; accessing a
+route reserved for the other role returns `403`.
+
+### Team invitations and user management
+
+All paths below are relative to `/api/v1` and require `X-Helpdesk-Account` and an
+active account. People management is admin-only, not owner-only. Agents can work
+on tickets and read staff choices but cannot manage users or invitations.
+
+| Method/path | Access | Successful response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /invitations` | Admin | `201 {"invitation":…}` |
+| `GET /invitations?status=pending` | Admin | `200 {"invitations":[…],…pagination}` |
+| `GET /invitations/:id` | Admin | `200 {"invitation":…}` |
+| `POST /invitations/:id/resend` | Admin | `200 {"invitation":…}` |
+| `DELETE /invitations/:id` | Admin | `204` (revokes, retains history) |
+| `POST /invitation_previews` | No session required | `200 {"invitation_preview":…}` |
+| `POST /invitation_acceptances` | No session required | `201 {"user":…, "account":…}` |
+| `GET /users?role=agent&suspended=false` | Admin | `200 {"users":[…],…pagination}` |
+| `GET /users/:id` | Admin | `200 {"user":…}` |
+| `PATCH /users/:id` | Admin | `200 {"user":…}` |
+| `GET /staff` | Staff | `200 {"staff":[…],…pagination}` |
+
+Create an invitation with
+`{"invitation":{"email":"sam@example.test","role":"agent"}}`. Only `admin` and
+`agent` can be invited. Emails are normalized; existing users and live pending
+invitations reserve their email within the account. Customer registration cannot
+claim an email reserved by a pending invitation.
+
+Invitation responses include `id`, `email`, `role`, `status`, `expires_at`,
+`accepted_at`, `revoked_at`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`, never the token or digest.
+Statuses are `pending`, `accepted`, `revoked`, and `expired`; expiry is reflected
+immediately without requiring a scheduled job. Invitations expire after seven days.
+The email points to
+`${HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL}/accept-invitation?account=acme&token=TOKEN`.
+Resending rotates the token and resets expiry; previous links stop working.
+Expired invitations can be resent if the email is still available. Accepted and
+revoked invitations cannot be resent; accepted invitations cannot be revoked.
+
+Preview using `{"invitation_preview":{"token":"TOKEN"}}`. The response envelope
+contains `email`, `role`, `business_name`, `subdomain`, and `expires_at`. Accept using
+`{"invitation_acceptance":{"token":"TOKEN","name":"Sam","password":"my-team-password"}}`.
+Acceptance is atomic and single-use, creates a verified user with the invited role,
+and does not send another verification email. The user can then sign in through
+`POST /sessions`. Invalid, expired, revoked, already-used, and wrong-account tokens
+return `422` for both preview and acceptance.
+
+Users include a boolean `suspended`. Update it with
+`{"user":{"suspended":true}}`; only this field is writable through the admin user
+endpoint. Suspension immediately deletes sessions and clears ticket assignments,
+blocks further sign-in, and removes the user from staff choices. Unsuspending does
+not restore old sessions or assignments. The last verified, unsuspended admin
+cannot be suspended. Staff choices include verified, unsuspended staff only.
+
+User, staff, and invitation lists use ID ascending and the existing pagination
+format. User filters `role` and `suspended` can be combined; invitation lists accept
+`status`. Invalid filters return `400`.
+
+### Assignment and internal activity
+
+Staff can assign a ticket using `{"ticket":{"assignee_id":12}}` or clear it using
+`{"ticket":{"assignee_id":null}}`. Assignees must be verified, unsuspended staff
+in the same account. Staff ticket representations include `assignee_id` and
+`assignee: {"id":…, "name":…}` (both null when unassigned); customer request
+representations do not expose assignment data.
+
+Staff can add a private note using
+`{"ticket":{"comment":{"body":"Checking this with billing.","public":false}}}`.
+Assignment, status, and comment changes in a single PATCH are atomic. Private notes
+appear only in staff comment lists. Private notes, assignment changes, and assignment
+clearing on suspension leave customer request representations and ordering unchanged.
+
+### Cancellation and cleanup
+
+Cancellation sets `account.cancelled_at` and immediately revokes every account
+session. Public portal and ordinary authenticated access are blocked. Within
+90 days, only the owner may sign in to obtain a session for reactivation (or logout).
+The owner can recover a forgotten password during this window. Other admins cannot
+cancel or reactivate the account. Repeated cancellation does not extend the deadline.
+At the 90-day boundary reactivation and sign-in are denied, even before cleanup runs.
+
+Schedule this command daily (e.g. cron or a deployment scheduler):
+
+```sh
+bin/rails helpdesk:purge_cancelled_accounts
+```
+
+It permanently deletes accounts cancelled for **at least** 90 days, including all
+users, invitations, token digests, sessions, tickets, and comments. Active, recently cancelled, and reactivated
+accounts are untouched. Account row locks serialize lifecycle requests with cleanup;
+the task rechecks eligibility under the lock. New account-owned data models should
+also have cascading foreign keys and dependent associations. Retention for external
+SMTP logs and database backups must be configured separately by the operator.
+
+## Verification
+
+```sh
+bin/test
+# Or use your own separate PostgreSQL test database:
+TEST_DATABASE_URL='postgresql://localhost/helpdesk_test' bin/test
+# Equivalent direct Rails invocation:
+RAILS_ENV=test DATABASE_URL='postgresql://localhost/helpdesk_test' bin/rails db:prepare test
+bin/rubocop
+bin/brakeman --no-pager
+```
+
+`bin/test` explicitly selects a separate test database instead of inheriting the
+development `DATABASE_URL`. Integration tests exercise the full JSON lifecycle,
+email delivery and token extraction, tenant isolation, expiry, session revocation,
+owner-only cancellation, cleanup, validation errors, and CORS. Customer conversation
+tests also cover registration and recovery, role/tenant/requester isolation, public
+comment visibility, atomic reply/status updates, activity ordering, pagination, and
+email-free replies.
+
+For public deployment, configure authentication/email-request rate limits at the
+API gateway and monitor SMTP failures. No frontend application or scheduled job
+runner is included.
+
+## Email support (Action Mailbox)
+
+Businesses receive mail at `<subdomain>@<HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN>` (for example,
+`acme@support.example.test`). `HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN` defaults to
+`support.example.test`. Configure your mail server/MX to relay mail for the whole
+support domain, including plus-addresses, without rewriting the To/Cc recipients.
+
+Run `bin/rails db:prepare` with `DATABASE_URL` set. Set a strong
+`RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD`, then forward raw RFC 822 messages via HTTPS:
+
+```sh
+curl --user "actionmailbox:$RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD" \
+  --header 'Content-Type: message/rfc822' \
+  --data-binary @message.eml \
+  https://api.example.com/rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails
+```
+
+Rails uses HTTP Basic authentication with username `actionmailbox`. If an
+`action_mailbox.ingress_password` credential exists, Rails gives it precedence;
+remove it when using the environment variable. Keep the relay password on the
+mail server only, not in the frontend. In test, routing runs inline before the
+request returns; no worker is required. Other environments use Active Job's
+configured adapter. Use a durable job backend/worker for production. Raw messages
+use Active Storage (`storage/` locally, `tmp/storage/` in test); production must
+provide persistent storage or configure an appropriate shared storage service.
+Processed raw messages follow Action Mailbox's default incineration policy.
+
+New email creates a ticket and public plain-text comment. To/Cc (and other
+recipients exposed by Action Mailbox) are considered; mail for multiple known
+businesses is ignored. Automated/list mail, HTML-only messages, empty content,
+unknown destinations, cancelled businesses, and suspended or non-customer
+senders are ignored without creating customers or consuming Message-IDs.
+Multipart mail uses the non-attachment plain-text part; attachments are not
+imported into conversations.
+
+An existing tenant customer is reused. A new customer receives the existing
+verification email and remains **unverified** until they follow its link. Their
+initial password is random; they can use the portal's password-reset flow to set
+a password. Receiving mail never verifies an account.
+
+A public staff reply to an email-origin ticket is mailed to its requester, with
+an opaque, private `Reply-To` address at the support domain. Private notes and
+portal-origin tickets do not generate these notifications. Only the requester's
+email address may reply; such replies reopen pending/solved tickets. Replies to closed tickets are rejected
+without adding a comment or reopening either side of a merge. A message
+to the plain business address always starts a new ticket, regardless of subject
+or threading headers. Reply tokens are not included in API ticket JSON.
+Configure outbound SMTP using the existing `SMTP_*` settings and authorize the
+support domain in your mail provider's SPF/DKIM configuration. The trusted relay
+should enforce its anti-spoofing policy: a From header alone is not cryptographic
+proof of sender identity.
+
+Accepted Message-IDs are recorded transactionally with the conversation, with a
+unique database index. Duplicate deliveries (including changed raw copies) do
+not add messages. Invalid deliveries do not reserve an ID, and identical rejected
+or failed raw deliveries may be retried. Accepted-ID receipts outlive raw-message
+incineration so old deliveries cannot recreate tickets.
+
+Run the email integration tests against a dedicated test database:
+
+```sh
+DATABASE_URL=postgresql:///helpdesk_test bin/rails db:prepare RAILS_ENV=test
+DATABASE_URL=postgresql:///helpdesk_test bin/rails test test/integration/inbound_email_test.rb
+```
+
+### Staff groups and memberships
+
+Groups organize tickets within an account; they do **not** restrict staff visibility.
+All endpoints below use the `/api/v1` prefix and the existing bearer token and
+`X-Helpdesk-Account` headers. Customers cannot access these resources. Staff may
+list and view them; only admins may create, rename, delete, or change defaults.
+
+| Method | Path | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| GET / POST | `/groups` | List / create groups |
+| GET / PATCH / DELETE | `/groups/:id` | View / rename / delete a group |
+| GET / POST | `/group_memberships` | List / create memberships |
+| GET / DELETE | `/group_memberships/:id` | View / remove a membership |
+| PUT | `/group_memberships/:id/make_default` | Make this the user's default membership |
+
+Create or rename a group with `{"group":{"name":"Billing"}}`. Create a
+membership with
+`{"group_membership":{"group_id":20,"user_id":12,"default":true}}`.
+`default` is optional; the first membership always becomes the default. Only
+staff from the same account can be members. Each user/group pair is unique.
+Setting a default replaces the previous default; deleting it promotes the
+remaining membership with the lowest ID. Removing the last membership leaves
+no default. Suspension preserves memberships and defaults.
+
+Lists use the existing pagination envelope, ordered by ID ascending. Membership
+lists accept `group_id` and `user_id` filters, which may be combined. Singular
+responses use `group` or `group_membership`; deletes return 204.
+
+Staff ticket responses now include `group_id` and `group` (`{"id":20,"name":"Billing"}`
+or `null`). `GET /tickets?group_id=20` filters by group;
+`GET /tickets?group_id=unassigned` selects tickets without a group. These filters
+combine with the existing inbox filters.
+
+Use `PATCH /tickets/:id` with `{"ticket":{"group_id":20}}` to move a ticket.
+An assignee must be active staff and belong to the ticket's group. An incompatible
+explicit assignment is rejected. Assigning a person to an ungrouped ticket uses
+their default group when available; staff without memberships can still be
+assigned to ungrouped tickets. Moving only the group clears an incompatible
+existing assignee. Clearing `assignee_id` leaves the group unchanged. Explicitly
+setting `group_id` to `null` clears both fields, even if an assignee was also sent.
+Deleting a membership clears that user's assignments in that group; deleting a
+group clears both fields on its tickets. Neither operation deletes tickets.
+Customer request responses and writable fields remain unchanged.
